## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design an incremental data structure for computing a non-trivial graph statistic under streaming batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental degree-based metrics
- Amortized analysis for batch/streaming updates
- Space-time trade-offs in dynamic graph algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
let deg = Array(n).fill(0);
let P = 0;

function addEdge(u, v) {
  const du = deg[u], dv = deg[v];
  P += 2 * (du + dv);
  deg[u] = du + 1;
  deg[v] = dv + 1;
}

function processBatch(edges) {
  edges.forEach(([u, v]) => addEdge(u, v));
  return P;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle edge deletions?
- How does multi-graph handling (parallel edges) affect this approach?
- What are the time and space complexities compared to recomputing from scratch?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming heavy-hitters and memory-accuracy trade-offs in a realistic KPI scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Heavy hitters (Misra-Gries)
- Online updates, fixed memory, approximate top-k
- Error bounds depend on total decrements; can be verified with a secondary pass

## Code Example
```javascript
class MGTopK { constructor(k){ this.k=k; this.counters=new Map(); }
  offer(x){ // update logic...
    for (let [v,c] of this.counters){ if (v===x){ this.counters.set(v,c+1); return; } }
    if (this.counters.size < this.k){ this.counters.set(x,1); return; }
    for (let v of Array.from(this.counters.keys())) this.counters.set(v, this.counters.get(v)-1);
    for (let [v,c] of Array.from(this.counters)) if (c<=0) this.counters.delete(v);
  }
  topK(){ return Array.from(this.counters.entries()).sort((a,b)=>b[1]-a[1]).slice(0,this.k); }
}}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle ties in top-k?
- How to validate accuracy on real data?
- How would this adapt if k changes dynamically?

